WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.482
In the hall of a busy house hangs a board with today's menu.

2
00:00:06.482 --> 00:00:10.265
Walk past, glance at it, and you know what's for dinner now.

3
00:00:10.265 --> 00:00:12.597
Nobody reads yesterday's menu off it.

4
00:00:12.800 --> 00:00:14.433
In the kitchen hangs a bell.

5
00:00:14.433 --> 00:00:19.506
When dinner's ready, the cook rings it, and everyone listening hears it at that moment.

6
00:00:19.506 --> 00:00:21.780
Whoever comes home later hears nothing.

7
00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:25.558
Last lesson, a flow cooked nothing until someone collected.

8
00:00:25.558 --> 00:00:28.212
A menu is there whether anyone looks or not.

9
00:00:28.212 --> 00:00:35.268
So here's a question that's easy to be shy about: if the menu changes three times while you're busy, what do you see?

10
00:00:36.100 --> 00:00:37.941
Kotlin's board is a StateFlow.

11
00:00:37.941 --> 00:00:41.869
It always has a value you can read at any moment, like a glance.

12
00:00:41.869 --> 00:00:45.490
Collect it, and you get that value first, then each change.

13
00:00:45.490 --> 00:00:48.927
And it's hot: it's there whether anyone collects or not.

14
00:00:49.633 --> 00:00:50.505
Let's try it.

15
00:00:50.505 --> 00:00:56.740
The board shows tomato soup, and the helper who chalks it up needs 100 milliseconds per menu.

16
00:00:56.740 --> 00:01:02.773
While tomato is being chalked up, the cook changes her mind three times: leek, pea, onion.

17
00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:05.527
Which soups does the board show?

18
00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:09.722
Tomato, then onion.

19
00:01:09.722 --> 00:01:11.973
Leek and pea were never chalked up.

20
00:01:11.973 --> 00:01:15.510
A slow collector doesn't get a queue of what it missed.

21
00:01:15.510 --> 00:01:18.211
When it's ready, it gets the latest value.

22
00:01:18.211 --> 00:01:19.819
That's called conflation.

23
00:01:20.033 --> 00:01:24.257
Then the cook sets onion again, a new object with the same fields.

24
00:01:24.257 --> 00:01:29.568
Nothing happens: a StateFlow compares with equals, and an equal value wakes nobody.

25
00:01:29.568 --> 00:01:33.024
Nobody needs a board to announce that nothing changed.

26
00:01:33.233 --> 00:01:36.940
That's perfect for state, where only the latest value matters.

27
00:01:36.940 --> 00:01:42.501
It's wrong for events, where every one matters, like each step of a dish for the kitchen log.

28
00:01:42.501 --> 00:01:45.072
Conflation would drop some without a trace.

29
00:01:45.900 --> 00:01:49.242
For events, the house needs the bell: a SharedFlow.

30
00:01:49.242 --> 00:01:50.815
It has no current value.

31
00:01:50.815 --> 00:02:04.053
It sends every value to everyone subscribed at that moment, and three settings decide what it keeps: replay, for late listeners; extraBufferCapacity, room for busy ones; and what to do when that's full.

32
00:02:04.267 --> 00:02:11.980
Now ring it five times with tryEmit, the version that never waits: if a ring doesn't fit, it returns false and walks off.

33
00:02:11.980 --> 00:02:17.208
One listener is there, and it needs 50 milliseconds to write each ring in the log.

34
00:02:17.433 --> 00:02:20.640
With the default settings, how many rings reach the log?

35
00:02:23.633 --> 00:02:23.993
None.

36
00:02:23.993 --> 00:02:27.728
tryEmit said false five times, and the log is empty.

37
00:02:27.728 --> 00:02:35.272
The default bell has no buffer at all, so tryEmit fails whenever anyone is subscribed, even someone idle.

38
00:02:35.272 --> 00:02:38.145
Five rings, one listener, nothing heard.

39
00:02:38.367 --> 00:02:42.621
Give it room for two: two rings get in, three are refused.

40
00:02:42.621 --> 00:02:49.810
Add DROP_OLDEST, and all five say true, but only e4 and e5 arrive: three were quietly thrown away.

41
00:02:49.810 --> 00:02:52.304
With room for 64, all five arrive.

42
00:02:52.304 --> 00:02:57.145
And a late listener to a bell with replay 2 hears e4 and e5 first.

43
00:02:57.367 --> 00:03:00.979
So give the bell a buffer, and check what tryEmit returns.

44
00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:03.677
Here the bell picture breaks.

45
00:03:03.677 --> 00:03:11.380
A real bell can't wait for someone in the shower; a buffered SharedFlow keeps each ring until a busy listener is ready.

46
00:03:11.380 --> 00:03:16.881
But in an empty house they agree: a ring nobody hears is gone, apart from the replay.

47
00:03:17.700 --> 00:03:19.459
Now last lesson's problem.

48
00:03:19.459 --> 00:03:33.329
A cold flow reads the oven thermometer every 100 milliseconds, and three screens around the house each collect it: three collectors, three runs, and the oven is read three times as often as it needs to be.

49
00:03:33.533 --> 00:03:43.948
stateIn runs the cold flow once, in a scope you own, and shares it with every collector: one cook, one cake, sliced for everyone.

50
00:03:43.948 --> 00:03:52.102
With WhileSubscribed of 300, two screens arrive at 327 milliseconds, and reading starts once, at 344.

51
00:03:52.102 --> 00:03:55.655
They leave at 581, and reading stops at 884.

52
00:03:55.867 --> 00:04:00.412
With Eagerly, reading started at 62 milliseconds, before anyone watched.

53
00:04:00.412 --> 00:04:07.293
A few seconds of WhileSubscribed is the usual choice, so a screen that restarts doesn't restart the readings.

54
00:04:08.100 --> 00:04:10.520
Last: who may write on the board?

55
00:04:10.520 --> 00:04:11.766
Only the kitchen.

56
00:04:11.766 --> 00:04:24.525
Since Kotlin 2.4, a property can hide a writable field behind a read-only type: outside the class, status is a StateFlow you can only read; inside, it's the MutableStateFlow.

57
00:04:24.733 --> 00:04:26.926
And write in one step.

58
00:04:26.926 --> 00:04:34.002
Eight helpers each count 100,000 plates with value equals value plus 1.

59
00:04:34.002 --> 00:04:35.795
Expected, 800,000.

60
00:04:35.795 --> 00:04:37.091
Got, 457,636.

61
00:04:37.091 --> 00:04:43.369
Two helpers read 41, both write 42, and a plate goes uncounted.

62
00:04:43.600 --> 00:04:47.109
Over 340,000 plates, washed and never counted.

63
00:04:47.109 --> 00:04:49.321
The dishwasher has questions.

64
00:04:49.321 --> 00:04:58.169
update retries with compare-and-set, changing the value only if nobody got there first, and gets 800,000 every time.

65
00:04:59.000 --> 00:05:05.300
Here's the board, the bell and the shared thermometer in Kotlin, with Reactor's sinks in Java below.

66
00:05:07.367 --> 00:05:09.727
So, three changes while you're busy?

67
00:05:09.727 --> 00:05:11.169
You see only the last.

68
00:05:11.169 --> 00:05:16.349
A StateFlow is the board: one current value, and nobody reads yesterday's menu.

69
00:05:16.349 --> 00:05:22.971
A SharedFlow is the bell: heard only by whoever listens, and its buffer decides which rings are lost.

70
00:05:22.971 --> 00:05:28.413
stateIn shares one run with many screens, and only the kitchen writes, in one step.

71
00:05:28.633 --> 00:05:29.545
Quick check.

72
00:05:29.545 --> 00:05:34.862
A StateFlow holds 0, and its collector takes 100 milliseconds a value.

73
00:05:34.862 --> 00:05:38.355
You set 1, 2 and 3 at once, and later 3 again.

74
00:05:38.355 --> 00:05:39.647
What does it see?

75
00:05:42.633 --> 00:05:43.232
0 and 3.

76
00:05:43.232 --> 00:05:48.321
1 and 2 were skipped, and the second 3 is equal, so it wakes nobody.
